		heading: 'What “consecrate” means',
		bodyHtml: `<p>To consecrate something is to <strong>set it apart for a holy purpose</strong>: to dedicate a person, a place or an object to our Father and give it no other use. A consecrated priest, altar or life now belongs to Him. <em>Consecration</em> is the act, and the state that follows is being holy, which simply means set apart for Him.</p><p>Two things are worth knowing at the start. First, the Bible never treats consecration as a ladder you climb to earn His attention. In Scripture our Father is the one doing the setting apart. Second, Father&rsquo;s Heart Bible does not print the word &ldquo;consecrate&rdquo; at all. It renders the same Hebrew and Greek with plainer English: <strong>dedicate</strong>, <strong>set apart</strong>, <strong>make holy</strong>, <strong>ordain</strong>. When you search for consecrate, those are the words to look for.</p>`,
