		bodyHtml: `<p>The main Hebrew verb is <em>qadash</em> (Strong&rsquo;s H6942). Blue Letter Bible&rsquo;s outline gives its sense as to set apart, to consecrate, to dedicate, to be holy. The English Bible most often renders it &ldquo;sanctify&rdquo; or &ldquo;hallow,&rdquo; and in the King James Version &ldquo;consecrate&rdquo; is one of its translations in five places. The root idea is separation <em>to</em> someone, not separation <em>from</em> everyone.</p><p>For installing a priest, Hebrew uses a vivid idiom: <em>male</em> (H4390) <em>yad</em>, literally <strong>&ldquo;to fill the hand.&rdquo;</strong> The same lexicon lists &ldquo;to consecrate, fill the hand&rdquo; among the senses of <em>male</em>. That is the phrase standing behind &ldquo;ordain&rdquo; in Exodus 29:9. The Greek counterpart is <em>hagiaz&#333;</em> (G37), from <em>hagios</em>, holy, defined as to separate from profane things and dedicate to God, to purify. It is the verb in John 17:17 and 17:19.</p>`,
